From c658e052d216c2ac7f31150a02ef6bdee671c40e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:55:35 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(blocks): render Bedrock state geometry and materials

Resolve custom block properties and ordered permutations after StartGame,
including per-face textures, geometry, transforms, shapes and lighting.
Reserve free note-block and redstone-wire states deterministically and deduplicate identical
visual and physical definitions. Preserve the original vanilla mapping
when a definition or a required custom texture cannot be represented.

Bedrock sends the runtime definitions after the initial pack. Keep Java
in configuration while it acknowledges a supplemental converted pack and
buffer ordered world packets with explicit count and byte bounds. Only
that pack's acknowledgement releases the scene; rejection clears custom
mappings before joining.

Saved Bedrock 1.26.51 scenes resolve all 276 CubeCraft states, all 1,795
Minehut states and all 199 Geyser states. Geyser mushroom faces use Java's corresponding vanilla
sprites when the server omits those vanilla Bedrock textures. Conversion
rejects ambiguous geometry and nonuniform scaling of rotated cubes. Legacy texture-array variation selection remains unimplemented.

Targeted tests cover permutations, transforms, face materials, lighting,
pack identity, rejection, queue bounds and one-shot completion.

Resume buffered packets with their Bedrock type after the base protocols that
already processed them. Preserve cancellation and downstream translation
without forcing either tracked connection state.

Bind the particle sprite explicitly for both full-cube and geometry block
models. The recorded Geyser scene exposed Java model warnings and missing
block particle textures despite valid face sprites.

Compose static parent-bone rotations child-to-root around their distinct
pivots and inflate cubes on both sides. Minehut's saved custom skulls need
these transforms and 1,790 distinct visuals, beyond the original 1,349
note-block carriers. Append 1,280 unoccupied, water-free wire states while
keeping note assignments stable and excluding every vanilla Bedrock
mapping. Preserve vanilla wire multipart selectors for unassigned states.
Native client adapters suppress carrier redstone particles and tint.

Exhaustive semantic tests cover all 1,296 wire states, exact custom/vanilla
partitions, compact selector conditions and ordinary note fallback.

Retain bone membership from the converter's remapped emitted group indices
when applying static block transforms. Invisible cubes emit no Java element
and must not shift later visible cubes into another bone. Regression
coverage combines invisible parent and child cubes with distinct rotated
bones, preserved face UVs, and immutable source geometry.

Decode custom light filtering from the serialized StartGame component
minecraft:block_light_filter.lightLevel. The official Bedrock 1.26.51.1
Hive capture (protocol 2193) sends a byte value of zero for cat tails,
willow leaves and transparent lamps; reading the creator JSON component
name made those blocks incorrectly default to fully opaque. Runtime
components come directly from network NBT, so retain the wire emission
field minecraft:light_emission.emission without speculative JSON aliases.
Network-NBT and permutation tests preserve transparent, partial, opaque,
emitting and omitted-component behavior.

Preserve per-face material face_dimming from the same target Hive capture.
Its serialized material instances contain 136 explicit false values.
Java 26.3 ignores the older shade flag, so use shade_direction_override
with the upward direction to remove directional attenuation while retaining
world light and ambient occlusion. Partition mixed-face elements without
changing their bounds, rotations, UVs, emission, or solid-block occlusion.

Render missing back faces for zero-thickness alpha-test block planes while
retaining the authored texture at each corner, face lighting and static
rotations. Preserve explicitly authored opposite faces, single-sided
materials, inflated planes and solid cubes. The official Bedrock 1.26.51.1
Hive recording (protocol 2193) sends red snapdragons as crossed WEST-only
planes with alpha_test and disabled face dimming. Its broad plane faces
away from the matched camera; Java culled the native-visible blossoms.
Semantic tests cover every face axis, signed UVs and quarter-turn UV
rotations, material partitions and immutable source geometry.

Recognize minecraft:geometry.full_block_v1 and preserve its bottom face UV
rotation. The pinned Bedrock 1.26.51.1 server (build 51061372, protocol
2193) serializes this identifier for a format 1.21.80 full-block definition.
Treating it as an external model silently discarded the custom visual and
skipped the supplemental pack. Microsoft documents the compatibility
mapping and the 180-degree bottom-face difference introduced in 1.26.0:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/minecraft/creator/reference/content/blockreference/examples/blockcomponents/minecraftblock_geometry

Tests compare complete modern and legacy models after removing only the
expected bottom rotation, including simple and structured geometry fields,
collision, selection and opaque occlusion.

Transport protocol-bound collision, selection, occlusion and lighting
properties in the accepted supplemental Java resource pack. ViaProxy
clients cannot access ResourcePackStorage in the proxy process. Include
these properties in the converted-pack fingerprint so changes to physics
invalidate an otherwise identical model cache entry.

Retain the target VoxelShapes registry before StartGame with atomic, bounded
decoding. Bedrock 1.26.51.1 (protocol 2193) emits packet 337; a procedural
four-box fixture establishes X/Y/Z cell order with Z varying fastest and
low-bit-first packed occupancy. This registry describes culling, not
collision or selection. Rendering its culling rules remains incomplete.
Malformed and truncated payload tests preserve the previous registry.

Compile authored block-culling rules into individual model faces and export
transformed voxel face slices in the accepted supplemental resource pack.
Preserve bone/cube indices through invisible geometry and preserve block
identity only when a pack uses identity-based conditions, so ordinary
visual deduplication remains available. Retain opaque-neighbor controls
and default, block, permutation and culling-layer conditions.

The target 1.26.51 protocol carries voxel shapes before StartGame. Microsoft
documents culling rules and voxel face comparisons independently from
physical boxes. Numerical coverage tests retain holes and extents across
unequal grids. Quarter-turn tests align authored rules and native slices.
Vanilla partial-block shape mappings and full native comparisons still
require verification.
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/minecraft/creator/documents/voxelshapes
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/minecraft/creator/reference/content/blockcullingreference/examples/blockcullingrules/block_culling

Native Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372 content diagnostics reject an
explicit default condition and accept only the three named conditions.
Represent default behavior by omitting condition in authored JSON. Keep
DEFAULT in generated metadata, where it denotes that omitted condition.
Compact adjacent grid cells before client comparisons without filling
holes. Dense-grid tests preserve missing cells.

Native local-world comparisons preserve the different-permutation cube
and retain voxel geometry beside a transparent built-in cube without
culling rules. Only authored default rules participate in voxel slices.
Do not infer participation from a built-in unit shape or conditional
rules. Preserve implicit full-cube opaque-neighbor culling independently.

The built-in full_block model has no authored bone graph. Do not invent
a block bone for user culling rules. Native transparent built-in cubes
remain outside the custom voxel comparison, while an authored custom
geometry with a default opposite rule occludes the same half-grid.
Keep both cases distinct in compiled participation metadata.

Omit faces whose cutout or blended material has no visible pixels after alpha conversion. Java 26.3 assigns zero-area UV ranges to opaque rendering, which showed dark faces for fully transparent neighbors beside the pinned native culling fixture. Keep opaque materials visible and compile authored culling participation before omitting any faces, so invisible geometry retains its physical boxes and voxel coverage. Resolve the actual sprite on copied back faces and mixed material elements.

Treat zero-volume collision and selection boxes as empty physical shapes.
Do this before rotation so an enclosing AABB cannot make an empty shape
solid, and after transformation for collapsed axes. Canonical metadata
removes empty boxes without rejecting resource-pack conversion or its
cache fingerprint. Nonfinite and inverted metadata bounds remain errors.
Regression tests cover all three empty axes, rotated models, metadata
round trips and cache identity. The reported server values still need
a capture before attributing its exact failure to this case.

Retain position-seeded random offsets in visual deduplication, accepted
server-pack metadata and cache identity. Match the 1.26.51.1 build 51061372
sampling instructions, including uint32 steps, fixed axis draws and float
operation order. The production sampler matches 8,192 native outputs and
16,384 height-invariance comparisons without differences.

Matching-build BDS sends valid ranges in pixel units and rejects fixture
ranges that move collision outside its accepted bounds. Divide endpoints
by 16 at component loading without inventing a client clamp. Keep floored
world component collision separate from continuous selection and camera
translation; native wrappers use different paths. Metadata format 2 makes
those definitions available through ViaProxy as well as direct sessions.
This supplies custom component shapes, not a complete native movement
solver or vanilla per-type collision provider.

Retain the material AO exponent on each authored face in the converted
model, including aliases and generated reverse-winding planes. One face
with exponent zero must not disable AO on all other faces. Bedrock
1.26.51.1 retains a float exponent and applies it to the product of
corner ambient light and directional dimming, before tint and lightmap.
Native instruction probes cover six faces and 2,304 corner outputs with
unchanged light coordinates. The core carries metadata on both routes;
faithful per-face evaluation requires client renderer integration.

Native corner light takes independent maxima across block and sky
channels, without Java's zero replacement or arithmetic average.
Expose that calculation in the shared lighting helper so client terrain
integration can retain native corner brightness on both routes.
A private full-routine fixture verifies 12,288 heterogeneous corner
samples across six directions, face planes and opaque diagonal controls.
Neighborhood lookup and visible comparisons remain separate parity work.

Interpolate shaded terrain corners along one axis and then the other,
preserving the target native float operation order. A four-term weighted
sum can change a final vertex color by one level. Keep the arithmetic in
core for reuse; client terrain integration chooses the face axes.

Private executable probes run all six original native face emission
functions with supplied cached colors and cuboid bounds. They verify
submitted vertex positions and packed colors, including the different
axis grouping on horizontal and vertical faces. They do not establish
native world lookup, neighbor-to-color assignment, or visible parity.

Decode native fixture light with sky in the low byte and block light in
the high byte. Executing the original target world-light reader confirms
that layout and clamps only block light to the source emission floor.
The earlier fixture decoder swapped the channels; independent maxima
commute with that swap, so the corner tests alone could not detect it.

Add an optional independent world-read reference to retain the physical
channel binding across negative coordinates, compact light arrays and
emission floors. These references originate from actual core light
arrays supplied to native chunk records; they verify reading and fixture
conversion, not native propagation or complete visible parity.

Select terrain sides and diagonals in the native sampled plane, use
outside-normal ambient brightness even on inset faces, and choose source
light from the outside normal only for outer opaque faces. Keep these
rules in a renderer-independent sampler alongside physical corner mapping.
Apply authored emission to block light without raising skylight.

Execute the original 1.26.51.1 corner routine for all six directions,
inner/outer planes, both source opacity flags, every side blocker mask,
and heterogeneous channel/ambient patterns. All 12,288 corner selections
match. Original tessellator constants establish face tolerances of
0.0005 and 0.9995. Cached solidity, geometry-full initialization, per-type
ambient overrides, and visible native comparison remain separate work.

Parse native item atlas object texture paths and object entries inside arrays. Targeted atlas tests preserve path precedence and invalid entry handling.

Retain indexed terrain atlas variants for callers that select a Bedrock auxiliary value. Scalar server overrides apply to every variant, while the existing first-path block API keeps its behavior. Native pane item conversion needs this lookup for all sixteen stained-glass colors.

Separate supplemental-pack acknowledgement from the bounded world packet
buffer. After Java login and bootstrap, wait for the existing client ping
acknowledgement before translating the scene. Preserve packet order across
both buffering phases and ignore stale or disconnected acknowledgements.
Pre-StartGame skins still drain before this second phase.

The pinned Fabric networking API 6.3.8 registers play channels only after
ClientboundLoginPacket. Minecraft 26.3 handles the following ping on that
same client packet thread before sending pong. The recorded Bedrock
1.26.51.1 Enchanted lobby sends custom particles during this startup window;
previously translation dropped them before the native channel registered.
This also applies when the server has no supplemental custom-block pack.
Java-only clients keep their existing translation fallbacks after pong.

Tests preserve ordered resource/world delivery, one-shot release, channel
registration before release, replacement/disconnect isolation and bounds.

Resolve compiled item block references against immutable native primary
registrations before aliases and namespace fallback. Retain the target's
64 registry aliases and preserve exact primary ownership before ASCII
normalization. Compare namespace keys in unsigned UTF-8 order.

A matching target registry/alias fixture verifies 14 simple-name cases.
It rejects tile.stone: that saved-item prefix must not be stripped in the
shared registry lookup used by compiled component data. The compiled
block reader forwards its raw HashedString to the same native lookup.
The fixture supplies primary/alias tables and CRT/TLS boundaries; native
installation and namespace fallback remain unverified. Semantic tests
also cover immutable inputs and namespace precedence.

Retain native custom-state runtime slots independently of serialized
property equality. Repeated enum values, repeated definitions and aliased
multiblock ordinals must not remove earlier numeric or hashed runtime
entries. Probe occupied hash successors until each runtime entry is unique.

Expand trait domains before custom domains using sparse auxiliary bit
positions, capped at the native ushort palette. Preserve typed connection
bytes, native direction ordinals and multiblock serializer aliases. Trait
registration retains the first exact raw name while reflected lookup folds
ASCII case. Reproduce its raw-name bucket insertion and rehash ordering;
skip unknown reflected factories rather than disconnecting the client.

Private executable checks use Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372: 600 typed
legacy-ID reads, 12 sparse layouts with 131,222 constructed metadata slots,
788 trait flag cases, 13 serialized trait domains, all 256 multiblock
ordinals, and 37 raw-name maps including 256 case variants and rehashing.
These probes execute native functions with supplied storage, constructor
context and allocator/CRT boundaries. Trait component wiring and complete
network property decoding remain outside their scope. Hash successor
uniqueness has Java regression coverage, not a native hash-catalog proof.

Retain canonical block-item descriptors alongside connection runtime IDs.
Read builtin Legacy IDs and state ordering from the existing pinned block
palette, then reconstruct sparse auxiliary positions from each domain's
first transition. Avoid a second per-block ID/offset table. Validate native
serialized value prefixes against 118 property domains acquired from the
original target serializers, including chalkboard direction ordinals0..15
beyond that property's default count of four.

Keep custom descriptor ownership by exact state identity and original
sparse ushort metadata, including equal serialized states. Expose runtime
state descriptor lookup for the cooking catalog. Do not assign generic
metadata to a block that declares an archetype. Saved-item metadata lookup,
builtin default overrides and network Item construction remain separate.

Resolve block-item auxiliary values through registered native defaults,
curated target variants, and the existing six-bit legacy state mappings.
Use shared primary/alias/namespace lookup, keeping serialization names
separate. Metadata outside0..63 and non-minecraft namespaces use the exact
registered default, preserving custom state0 identity despite equal slots.
Canonicalize upgraded legacy values back to their owned palette objects.
Expose exact registered runtime IDs independently of latest logical lookup.

A pinned BDS1.26.51.1 ScriptAPI2.9.0 fixture observes all1,477 block defaults
and26 nonzero palette defaults after excluding virtual legacy accessors.
A separate command/inventory sweep verifies4,950 cases across75 available
block item types and corrects63 glow-lichen auxiliary mappings. The target
reference retains104 auxiliary definitions and64 serialization names.
26 attempted types are unavailable to the command interface. These server
checks do not prove the Windows client's cold cache, remaining curated
variants or custom archetype construction. Packet Item construction and
cooking presentation integration remain separate work.

Keep runtime BlockLegacy numeric ownership separate from canonical auxiliary
encoding. Custom archetypes retain their supplied legacy ID even when their
descriptor metadata is unverified, so valid runtime blocks are not rejected
merely because recipe conversion is incomplete.

Resolve item block restrictions through the target 1.26.51.1 registry
groups before exact primary names and aliases. Native resolution is
case-sensitive, retains a group prefix when a member is absent, and
deduplicates BlockLegacy identities. Keep these semantics separate from
ordinary folded block lookup. The 58 installed builtin groups come from
the original target initializer and registry installation routines.
Compare canonical registered identities in Java; native pointer-hash
collisions and dynamic native factory installation remain unverified.
